    "summary": "Punchy Loser, tight China>Rider and energetic Good Lovin'",
    "blurb": "Features strong performances of Loser, Easy Wind and the seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition. The second set maintains momentum with Bird Song, a driving Good Lovin', and a mellow Ripple finale. Overall the show is high‑energy with clear soundboard capture.",
    "review": "The Capitol Theater show on February 21, 1971 finds the Dead navigating a pivotal moment—Mickey Hart had just left, leaving Bill Kreutzmann to hold down the beat. The first set opens with a crisp, punchy Loser and an inspired Easy Wind before slipping into a high‑octane stretch that includes Playing In The Band, Bertha and a rousing Sugar Magnolia. Though Jerry's guitar sits slightly back in the early mix, his leads cut through on the standout numbers, especially Good Lovin' later in the night.\n\nThe second set is where the show truly shines. Bob Weir coaxed the crowd into a coyote‑howl before launching into a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider that feels both tight and exploratory. Bird Song glides beautifully, and the rhythm section—Phil’s bomb‑like bass and Bill’s solid drumming—keeps everything driving forward through Cumberland Blues, Wharf Rat and a double‑take on Good Lovin'. The night closes with a gentle Ripple, leaving the audience in a reflective mood. All of this is captured on an excellent soundboard mix that balances clarity with the room's love‑filled ambience.",
